"Groups stakeholders by their ability to influence an initiative and their interest in its outcomes.\n\nUse when: Plan proportionate stakeholder engagement. Identify influential parties whose concerns need early attention.\n\nAvoid when: Power is being mistaken for moral importance or a complete account of stakeholder needs.\n\nVisual grammar: Two axes describe defined power and interest levels. Four regions guide differentiated engagement approaches. Named stakeholder markers include dated evidence or uncertainty where useful.\n\nBuild: Define the initiative, impact boundary and meaning of each axis. Identify affected groups and assess power and interest with evidence. Choose engagement actions and review placements as circumstances change.\n\nCheck: Interest refers to the initiative rather than general enthusiasm. Power includes relevant informal influence as well as titles. Low-power affected groups remain included in engagement planning.\n\nPitfalls: Treating placements as permanent personality judgments. Ignoring vulnerable stakeholders because they have little formal power.\n\nExample: A park renovation places residents, accessibility advocates, funders and permitting officials to plan consultation and decision briefings.\n\nAlternatives: Stakeholder onion map, Service ecosystem map."
